---
// Page 11 — /holiday-home-management-dubai/permit/ · SUPPORT · Template C
// Blocks: B1 answer · B2 process · B3 requirements · B4 the rule · B5 FAQ · B6 cross-link
import PageShell from '../../../layouts/PageShell.astro';
import DataTable from '../../../components/DataTable.astro';
import FaqList from '../../../components/FaqList.astro';
import ValuationCrossLink from '../../../components/ValuationCrossLink.astro';
import NeedsSource from '../../../components/NeedsSource.astro';
import { byPath } from '../../../lib/pages.js';

const page = byPath('/holiday-home-management-dubai/permit/');

const answer =
  'You cannot legally short-let a Dubai property without a holiday home permit issued by the ' +
  'Department of Economy and Tourism. The permit is held per unit, renewed on a fixed cycle, ' +
  'and operating without one carries penalties. Landlords may register directly or let a ' +
  'licensed operator hold the permit for them.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Do I need a licence to short-let in Dubai?',
    a:
      'Yes. A holiday home permit from the Department of Economy and Tourism is required ' +
      'before a unit is advertised or let on a short-term basis. It is issued per unit, not ' +
      'per owner.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a management company hold the permit instead of me?',
    a:
      'A licensed holiday home operator can register units under their own licence, which is ' +
      'the route most owners take. The trade-off is that the permission then sits with the ' +
      'operator, so check what happens to it if you change operator.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens if I short-let without a permit?',
    a:
      'Operating without a permit is a breach of the holiday home regulations and carries ' +
      'penalties. Listings platforms also require a permit number in several jurisdictions, so ' +
      'the practical consequence often arrives before the regulatory one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How often does the permit need renewing?',
    a:
      'On a fixed cycle set by the Department of Economy and Tourism. Diarise the renewal ' +
      'rather than waiting to be reminded — an expired permit means the unit cannot legally be ' +
      'let while bookings may already be in the calendar.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the permit apply to a room, or the whole unit?',
    a:
      'Holiday home permits are issued against the unit. Which unit types and tenures are ' +
      'eligible is set by the Department of Economy and Tourism and is worth confirming ' +
      'directly for your specific property.',
  },
];
---

<PageShell page={page} h1="Dubai holiday home permit" answer={answer} faqs={faqs}>
  <h2>What does the registration process involve?</h2>

  <DataTable
    caption="DET holiday home permit — process, cost and timing"
    columns={['Step', 'What happens', 'Published fee', 'Published timing']}
    rows={[
      [
        '1. Eligibility',
        'Confirm the unit type and tenure qualify under the holiday home rules',
        '—',
        '—',
      ],
      [
        '2. Application',
        'Register the unit with the Department of Economy and Tourism',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: DET application fee]</span>',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: DET stated processing time]</span>',
      ],
      [
        '3. Documentation',
        'Submit title deed, identification and unit details',
        '—',
        '—',
      ],
      [
        '4. Classification',
        'The unit is classified, which affects the tourism dirham charged per night',
        '—',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: DET classification criteria]</span>',
      ],
      [
        '5. Permit issued',
        'The permit number is issued and must appear where the unit is advertised',
        '—',
        '—',
      ],
      [
        '6. Renewal',
        'The permit is renewed on a fixed cycle',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: DET renewal fee]</span>',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: DET permit validity period]</span>',
      ],
    ]}
    source="Department of Economy and Tourism holiday home registration"
  />

  <p>
    Every fee and timing above is published by the Department of Economy and Tourism on its own
    pages. They are marked rather than approximated, because a permit fee a reader budgets
    against has to be the real one:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="DET published holiday home permit fees, validity period, renewal cycle and processing times" />
  </p>

  <h2>What documents are required?</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Title deed for the unit, or documentation establishing the right to let it</li>
    <li>Owner identification</li>
    <li>Unit details, including size and bedroom count</li>
    <li>DEWA account details for the unit</li>
    <li>Where an operator applies on your behalf, their licence and your authorisation</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The exact current list is set by the Department of Economy and Tourism and is the item most
    likely to have changed since any third-party guide was written, including this one. Check
    it against their own page before you assemble anything.
  </p>

  <h2>What is the rule?</h2>

  <p>
    This page quotes regulations rather than paraphrasing them, and the holiday home
    regulation — its number, its issuing authority and the wording of the permit requirement —
    has not been sourced to a primary document here:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="verbatim Dubai holiday home regulation, with number and issuing authority, plus the stated penalties for operating without a permit" />
  </p>

  <p>
    What is stated above without a citation is the requirement itself, which is not in dispute:
    a permit is needed, it is issued per unit, and it is issued by the Department of Economy
    and Tourism.
  </p>

  <h2>Should you hold the permit, or should the operator?</h2>

  <p>
    Most owners let the operator hold it, because it removes the administration. The cost is
    portability: if the permission sits under the operator’s licence, moving to a different
    operator means the registration moves too. Ask what happens to the permit on termination
    before you sign, and get the answer in writing.
  </p>

  <p>
    What operators charge for taking that on is on{' '}
    <a href="/holiday-home-management-dubai/">holiday home management in Dubai</a>, and whether
    short-letting beats a tenant at all is worked through on{' '}
    <a href="/holiday-home-management-dubai/short-let-vs-long-let/">
      short-let versus long-term rental
    </a>.
  </p>

  <ValuationCrossLink
    before="If the permit process is enough to make you reconsider letting the unit at all, "
    anchor="what the property is worth today"
    after=" is the other half of that decision."
  />

  <FaqList faqs={faqs} />
</PageShell>
